  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Figure 3, \"The health labour market framework\". Across the top runs a banner for \"Economy, population and broader societal drivers\". Below it, the diagram divides into \"Education sector\" on the left and \"Labour market dynamics\" on the right. In the education sector, \"High school\" branches into \"Education in health\" and \"Education in other fields\". \"Education in health\" feeds the \"Pool of qualified health workers\" (with two-way \"Migration\" to and from \"Abroad\"), which in turn splits into \"Employed\", \"Unemployed\" and \"Out of labour force\". \"Employed\" flows into the \"Health care sector\" and \"Other sectors\" (which also receives workers from education in other fields). The \"Health care sector\" produces a \"Health workforce equipped to deliver quality health service\", leading to \"Universal health coverage with safe, effective, person-centred health services\". Beneath the flow sit three policy boxes aligned with the stages — \"Policies on production\" (on infrastructure and material, enrolment, selecting students, teaching staff), \"Policies to address inflows and outflows\" (to address migration and emigration, attract unemployed health workers, bring health workers back into the health care sector), and \"Policies to address maldistribution and inefficiencies\" (to improve productivity and performance, improve skill mix composition, retain health workers in underserved areas) — all spanned at the bottom by a fourth box, \"Policies to regulate the private sector\" (to manage dual practice, improve quality of training, enhance service delivery).",
      "lang": "en",
